/* Shortcut Press — 3D Modeling Fundamentals in Blender guide content (generated by tools/site/build.mjs). */
window.SP_GUIDE = {"id":"blender-3d-modeling","slug":"blender-3d-modeling-fundamentals","title":"3D Modeling Fundamentals in Blender","software":"Blender","apps":["Blender"],"intro":"Build and render your first 3D scenes in Blender: navigating the interface, modeling with basic shapes, modifiers, materials, lighting, and rendering a finished scene. The steps use Blender's default keymap and left-click selection.","platformNote":"Shortcuts are shown for Windows and Linux. On a Mac, use Cmd in place of Ctrl and Option in place of Alt. Most shortcuts work when the mouse pointer is over the 3D Viewport.","files":[{"name":"blockout-shapes.obj","title":"Blockout shapes","desc":"A cube, a cylinder and a sphere spaced apart, for the navigation and selection lesson.","size":"13 KB"},{"name":"stool-start.obj","title":"Stool starter","desc":"A seat and one leg, for practicing extrude, loop cuts and duplicates.","size":"1 KB"},{"name":"mug-half.obj","title":"Half a mug","desc":"One half of a mug body centered on the X axis, for the Mirror and Subdivision Surface modifiers.","size":"7 KB"},{"name":"room-scene.obj","title":"Room scene","desc":"A floor, two walls, a table and simple props, for the materials, lighting and rendering lessons.","size":"8 KB"}],"shortcuts":[{"keys":"G","action":"Move (grab) the selection","app":"Blender"},{"keys":"R","action":"Rotate the selection","app":"Blender"},{"keys":"S","action":"Scale the selection","app":"Blender"},{"keys":"Tab","action":"Switch between Object Mode and Edit Mode","app":"Blender"},{"keys":"Shift+A","action":"Open the Add menu","app":"Blender"},{"keys":"Shift+D","action":"Duplicate the selection","app":"Blender"},{"keys":"A","action":"Select all","app":"Blender"},{"keys":"Alt+A","action":"Deselect all","app":"Blender"},{"keys":"X","action":"Delete menu","app":"Blender"},{"keys":"E","action":"Extrude (Edit Mode)","app":"Blender"},{"keys":"I","action":"Inset faces (Edit Mode)","app":"Blender"},{"keys":"Ctrl+B","action":"Bevel edges (Edit Mode)","app":"Blender"},{"keys":"1","action":"Vertex select (Edit Mode)","app":"Blender"},{"keys":"2","action":"Edge select (Edit Mode)","app":"Blender"},{"keys":"3","action":"Face select (Edit Mode)","app":"Blender"},{"keys":"H","action":"Hide the selection","app":"Blender"},{"keys":"Alt+H","action":"Reveal hidden objects","app":"Blender"},{"keys":"N","action":"Toggle the sidebar","app":"Blender"},{"keys":"Z","action":"Viewport shading pie menu","app":"Blender"},{"keys":"Home","action":"Frame all objects in the view","app":"Blender"},{"keys":"Numpad1","action":"Front view","app":"Blender","note":"Needs a numeric keypad, or Emulate Numpad in Preferences."},{"keys":"Numpad3","action":"Right view","app":"Blender"},{"keys":"Numpad7","action":"Top view","app":"Blender"},{"keys":"Numpad0","action":"Look through the active camera","app":"Blender"},{"keys":"Ctrl+A","action":"Apply menu (Apply Scale and more)","app":"Blender"},{"keys":"F9","action":"Reopen the Adjust Last Operation panel","app":"Blender"},{"keys":"Ctrl+Z","action":"Undo","app":"Blender"},{"keys":"Ctrl+S","action":"Save the .blend file","app":"Blender"},{"keys":"Ctrl+R","action":"Loop cut (Edit Mode)","app":"Blender","practice":false,"note":"Your browser uses this shortcut to reload the page."},{"keys":"F12","action":"Render the image","app":"Blender","practice":false,"note":"Your browser may reserve this key for developer tools."}],"lessons":[{"id":"navigating","title":"Navigating the interface","summary":"Move around the 3D view, select objects, switch modes, and find the editors you'll use most.","diagram":{"type":"mesh","variant":"axes","title":"The three axes and the transform keys"},"steps":[{"title":"The main editors","body":"<p>Blender's default layout has four editors you'll use constantly:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>3D Viewport</strong> (the big area): where you see and edit the scene.</li>\n<li><strong>Outliner</strong> (top right): a list of every object; click a name to select it.</li>\n<li><strong>Properties editor</strong> (bottom right): tabs for render settings, the selected object, modifiers, materials and more.</li>\n<li><strong>Timeline</strong> (bottom): for animation; you can ignore it in this guide.</li></ul>\n<p>Shortcuts act on the editor under the mouse pointer, so keep the pointer over the 3D Viewport when pressing them.</p>"},{"title":"Orbit, pan and zoom","body":"<ul><li><strong>Orbit:</strong> hold the middle mouse button and drag.</li>\n<li><strong>Pan:</strong> hold <kbd>Shift</kbd> and the middle mouse button and drag.</li>\n<li><strong>Zoom:</strong> scroll the wheel.</li></ul>\n<p>The navigation gizmo in the top-right corner of the viewport does the same with a left-click drag; click an axis letter to snap to that view. On a laptop without a middle button, go to <strong>Edit &gt; Preferences &gt; Input</strong> and turn on <em>Emulate 3 Button Mouse</em> (then <kbd>Alt</kbd> + left-drag orbits), and <em>Emulate Numpad</em> if you have no numeric keypad.</p>"},{"title":"Standard views","body":"<p>On the numeric keypad: <kbd>Numpad1</kbd> front, <kbd>Numpad3</kbd> right, <kbd>Numpad7</kbd> top, <kbd>Numpad5</kbd> switches between perspective and orthographic, and <kbd>Numpad0</kbd> looks through the camera. <kbd>Home</kbd> frames everything; <kbd>Numpad.</kbd> frames just the selection. Hold <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> with 1, 3 or 7 for the opposite side.</p>"},{"title":"Select objects","body":"<p>Left-click selects an object; <kbd>Shift</kbd> + left-click adds to or removes from the selection. <kbd>A</kbd> selects everything and <kbd>Alt</kbd> <kbd>A</kbd> deselects. Drag on empty space to box-select. The last object selected is the <em>active</em> one, drawn with a lighter outline; many tools use it as the reference.</p>"},{"title":"Object Mode and Edit Mode","body":"<p>In <strong>Object Mode</strong> you move whole objects. Press <kbd>Tab</kbd> for <strong>Edit Mode</strong> to work on an object's vertices, edges and faces, and <kbd>Tab</kbd> again to go back. The mode drop-down at the top left of the viewport shows which mode you're in. <kbd>N</kbd> toggles the sidebar with exact location, rotation and scale values.</p>"}],"quiz":[{"q":"Which key switches between Object Mode and Edit Mode?","options":["E","Tab","M","Space"],"answer":1,"explain":"Tab toggles Edit Mode for the selected object."},{"q":"You have no middle mouse button. Where do you turn on mouse emulation?","options":["File > Preferences","Edit > Preferences > Input","View > Navigation","Render Properties"],"answer":1,"explain":"Emulate 3 Button Mouse and Emulate Numpad are in Edit > Preferences > Input."},{"q":"Which key frames every object in the 3D Viewport?","options":["Home","F","Numpad5","Ctrl+Space"],"answer":0,"explain":"Home fits all objects into view; Numpad period frames only the selection."}],"exercise":{"title":"Find your way around","task":"<p>Import <strong>blockout-shapes.obj</strong>, then look at it from the front, right and top, select only the sphere, and frame it.</p>","files":["blockout-shapes.obj"],"solution":["Start Blender with the default scene. Select the default cube and press X &gt; Delete if you don't want it.","<strong>File &gt; Import &gt; Wavefront (.obj)</strong>, choose blockout-shapes.obj, and select <em>Import Wavefront OBJ</em>.","With the pointer over the viewport, press <kbd>Numpad1</kbd>, <kbd>Numpad3</kbd> and <kbd>Numpad7</kbd> (or click X, Y and Z on the navigation gizmo).","Press <kbd>Alt</kbd> <kbd>A</kbd>, click the sphere (or its name in the Outliner), then press <kbd>Numpad.</kbd> to frame it. Press <kbd>Home</kbd> to see everything again."]}},{"id":"basic-shapes","title":"Modeling with basic shapes","summary":"Add basic shapes, move, rotate and scale them, and shape them further with extrude, inset and loop cuts.","diagram":{"type":"mesh","variant":"extrude","title":"Extruding a face"},"steps":[{"title":"Add a shape","body":"<p>Press <kbd>Shift</kbd> <kbd>A</kbd> &gt; <strong>Mesh</strong> and choose Cube, UV Sphere, Cylinder or another primitive. It appears at the 3D cursor (the red and white target). Straight away, open the <strong>Adjust Last Operation</strong> panel in the bottom-left corner of the viewport (or press <kbd>F9</kbd>) to set its size, number of vertices and position before doing anything else.</p>"},{"title":"Move, rotate and scale","body":"<p>Press <kbd>G</kbd>, <kbd>R</kbd> or <kbd>S</kbd>, move the mouse, and left-click to confirm (right-click or <kbd>Esc</kbd> cancels). Add <kbd>X</kbd>, <kbd>Y</kbd> or <kbd>Z</kbd> to lock to an axis, and type a number for an exact amount: <kbd>S</kbd> <kbd>Z</kbd> <kbd>0</kbd><kbd>.</kbd><kbd>5</kbd> <kbd>Enter</kbd> halves the height.</p>\n<p>After scaling in Object Mode, press <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> <kbd>A</kbd> &gt; <strong>Scale</strong> to apply it. Bevels and modifiers behave unevenly on objects with unapplied scale.</p>"},{"title":"Edit vertices, edges and faces","body":"<p>In Edit Mode, press <kbd>1</kbd>, <kbd>2</kbd> or <kbd>3</kbd> (on the number row) for vertex, edge or face selection. G, R and S work on the selected parts. <kbd>X</kbd> opens the Delete menu, where you choose what to delete.</p>"},{"title":"Extrude, inset and loop cuts","body":"<ul><li><kbd>E</kbd> <strong>extrudes</strong> the selected faces, edges or vertices: it pulls out new geometry joined to the old.</li>\n<li><kbd>I</kbd> <strong>insets</strong> the selected faces, making a smaller face inside each one.</li>\n<li><kbd>Ctrl</kbd> <kbd>R</kbd> adds a <strong>loop cut</strong>: hover over an edge to preview the loop, scroll to add more cuts, click to confirm, then slide it or right-click to leave it centered.</li>\n<li><kbd>Ctrl</kbd> <kbd>B</kbd> <strong>bevels</strong> selected edges; scroll while dragging to add segments.</li></ul>"},{"title":"Duplicate and snap","body":"<p><kbd>Shift</kbd> <kbd>D</kbd> duplicates the selection and starts moving it; press an axis key to keep it in line. Hold <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> while moving to snap to the grid, or turn on snapping in the viewport header (the magnet).</p>"}],"quiz":[{"q":"Which keys scale the selection to half its height?","options":["S, Z, 0.5, Enter","G, Z, 0.5, Enter","S, X, 2, Enter","R, Z, 0.5, Enter"],"answer":0,"explain":"S scales, Z locks to the vertical axis, and 0.5 sets the factor."},{"q":"Why apply scale (Ctrl+A > Scale) after resizing an object?","options":["To save memory","So bevels and modifiers behave evenly","To make the object visible in renders","To unlock Edit Mode"],"answer":1,"explain":"Unapplied scale distorts tools such as Bevel that work in real distances."},{"q":"What does pressing I do with faces selected in Edit Mode?","options":["Insets them","Inserts a keyframe","Inverts the selection","Imports a file"],"answer":0,"explain":"In Edit Mode with faces selected, I insets them. (In Object Mode the same key inserts a keyframe.)"}],"exercise":{"title":"Finish the stool","task":"<p>Import <strong>stool-start.obj</strong>, which has a seat and one leg. Give the seat a rim with inset and extrude, then make three more legs so the stool stands evenly.</p>","files":["stool-start.obj"],"solution":["Import it with File &gt; Import &gt; Wavefront (.obj).","Select the seat, press <kbd>Tab</kbd>, then <kbd>3</kbd> for face select and click the top face.","Press <kbd>I</kbd> and move the mouse to inset about 0.05, click, then <kbd>E</kbd> and move down slightly to sink the middle, making a rim. Press <kbd>Tab</kbd> to leave Edit Mode.","Select the leg, press <kbd>Shift</kbd> <kbd>D</kbd>, <kbd>X</kbd>, and type <kbd>-</kbd><kbd>0</kbd><kbd>.</kbd><kbd>8</kbd>, then Enter. The leg is copied 0.8 m along X, which matches the seat's width in the file. Select both legs, press <kbd>Shift</kbd> <kbd>D</kbd>, <kbd>Y</kbd>, <kbd>-</kbd><kbd>0</kbd><kbd>.</kbd><kbd>8</kbd>, Enter.","Check from the front (<kbd>Numpad1</kbd>) and right (<kbd>Numpad3</kbd>) that all four legs reach the same height."]}},{"id":"modifiers","title":"Modifiers","summary":"Work non-destructively with Mirror, Subdivision Surface, Bevel and Array, stack them in order, and apply them when you're ready.","diagram":{"type":"stack","title":"A modifier stack runs from top to bottom","items":["Mirror","Subdivision Surface","Bevel"],"highlight":0},"steps":[{"title":"What modifiers do","body":"<p>A modifier changes how an object looks without changing its actual mesh, so you can adjust or remove it at any time. Add one in the <strong>Properties editor &gt; Modifier Properties</strong> tab (the wrench), then <strong>Add Modifier &gt; Generate</strong> and choose a modifier.</p>"},{"title":"Mirror","body":"<p><strong>Mirror</strong> reflects the mesh across the object's origin, so you only model one half. Pick the axis (usually X), and tick <em>Clipping</em> so vertices on the center line can't cross it. The object's origin must sit on the line of symmetry.</p>"},{"title":"Subdivision Surface","body":"<p><strong>Subdivision Surface</strong> smooths the mesh by splitting each face. <em>Levels Viewport</em> controls the preview and <em>Render</em> the final image; 2 is usually enough. In Object Mode, <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> <kbd>1</kbd> or <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> <kbd>2</kbd> adds the modifier at that level. Add loop cuts near an edge to keep it sharper. Use <strong>Object &gt; Shade Smooth</strong> to hide the faceting.</p>"},{"title":"Bevel and Array","body":"<p><strong>Bevel</strong> rounds edges: set <em>Amount</em> and <em>Segments</em>. Apply the object's scale first so the bevel is even. <strong>Array</strong> repeats the object: set <em>Count</em> and the <em>Relative Offset</em> (1 along X places copies side by side). Fences, shelves and steps are quick with Array.</p>"},{"title":"Order and applying","body":"<p>Modifiers run from the top of the stack down: Mirror then Subdivision Surface gives a smooth join along the middle; the reverse can leave a crease. Drag a modifier by its handle to reorder it. When you're sure, open its drop-down arrow and choose <strong>Apply</strong> (or hover over it and press <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> <kbd>A</kbd>) to make the change permanent.</p>"}],"quiz":[{"q":"In which order do modifiers run?","options":["Bottom to top","Top to bottom","Alphabetically","All at once"],"answer":1,"explain":"Each modifier works on the result of the one above it."},{"q":"The mirrored half of your model is offset from the original. What's the likely cause?","options":["The object's origin isn't on the line of symmetry","Subdivision is set too high","The material is missing","Clipping is on"],"answer":0,"explain":"Mirror reflects across the origin, so the origin has to sit on the center line."}],"exercise":{"title":"Mirror and smooth the mug","task":"<p>Import <strong>mug-half.obj</strong>, which is half of a mug body whose origin is already on the center line. Use modifiers to make the full, smooth mug, then apply them.</p>","files":["mug-half.obj"],"solution":["Import the file and select it.","Modifier Properties &gt; Add Modifier &gt; Generate &gt; <strong>Mirror</strong>. Keep Axis X and tick Clipping.","Add <strong>Subdivision Surface</strong> below it (or press <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> <kbd>2</kbd>). Check that Mirror is above Subdivision Surface.","Right-click the object and choose Shade Smooth (Object &gt; Shade Smooth).","Open the Mirror modifier's drop-down and choose Apply, then do the same for Subdivision Surface. The mug is now one complete mesh."]}},{"id":"materials","title":"Materials","summary":"Assign materials, set base color, roughness and metallic values, and give one object more than one material.","diagram":{"type":"compare","title":"How two Principled BSDF settings change a surface","left":{"title":"Roughness","items":["0.0: mirror-like gloss","0.3: polished plastic","0.6: satin paint","1.0: chalky and matte"]},"right":{"title":"Metallic","items":["0: plastics, wood, paint","1: bare metals","Base Color tints metal","Avoid values in between"]},"accentRight":true},"steps":[{"title":"Add a material","body":"<p>Select an object, open the <strong>Material Properties</strong> tab in the Properties editor, and select <strong>New</strong>. Blender creates a material using the <strong>Principled BSDF</strong> shader, which covers most real surfaces with a handful of settings. Name the material at the top so you can find it later.</p>"},{"title":"See materials in the viewport","body":"<p>Press <kbd>Z</kbd> for the shading pie menu and choose <strong>Material Preview</strong>, or use the shading buttons at the top right of the viewport. <em>Rendered</em> shading shows the final lighting but is slower.</p>"},{"title":"The key settings","body":"<ul><li><strong>Base Color:</strong> the surface color.</li>\n<li><strong>Metallic:</strong> 0 for non-metals (plastic, wood, paint), 1 for bare metal. Values in between rarely look real.</li>\n<li><strong>Roughness:</strong> 0 is mirror-smooth, 1 is completely matte. Most everyday surfaces sit between 0.3 and 0.7.</li></ul>"},{"title":"More than one material on an object","body":"<p>In Material Properties, select <strong>+</strong> to add a material slot and select <strong>New</strong> for a second material. Press <kbd>Tab</kbd>, select the faces that should use it, choose the slot and select <strong>Assign</strong>. To reuse an existing material, pick it from the browse drop-down next to its name instead of making a new one. To copy materials to other objects, select them, select the source object last, and press <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> <kbd>L</kbd> &gt; <strong>Link Materials</strong>.</p>"}],"quiz":[{"q":"What Metallic value suits a painted wooden table?","options":["0","0.5","1","It depends on the color"],"answer":0,"explain":"Paint and wood are non-metals, so Metallic should be 0."},{"q":"Which Roughness value gives a mirror-like shine?","options":["0","0.5","1","2"],"answer":0,"explain":"Lower roughness means a smoother, glossier surface."}],"exercise":{"title":"Give the room its materials","task":"<p>Import <strong>room-scene.obj</strong> and create four materials: matte floor, painted walls, a glossy table top, and a metal lamp. Give the table's legs a different material from its top.</p>","files":["room-scene.obj"],"solution":["Import the scene. Each part (Floor, Walls, Table, Lamp…) is a separate object in the Outliner.","Select Floor &gt; Material Properties &gt; New, name it Floor, set a mid-brown Base Color and Roughness 0.8.","Walls: a light color, Roughness 0.6. Lamp: a gray Base Color, Metallic 1, Roughness 0.3.","Table: create a material for the top (Roughness 0.2). Add a second slot with + and a New material for the legs. In Edit Mode, select the leg faces, choose the second slot, and select Assign.","Check with <kbd>Z</kbd> &gt; Material Preview."]}},{"id":"lighting","title":"Lighting","summary":"The different light types, a simple three-light setup, and using the world background to light a scene.","diagram":{"type":"lights"},"steps":[{"title":"Light types","body":"<p>Add a light with <kbd>Shift</kbd> <kbd>A</kbd> &gt; <strong>Light</strong>:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Point:</strong> shines in every direction from one spot, like a bare bulb.</li>\n<li><strong>Sun:</strong> parallel light from one direction; only its rotation matters, not its position.</li>\n<li><strong>Spot:</strong> a cone of light, like a stage light.</li>\n<li><strong>Area:</strong> light from a flat shape; bigger areas give softer shadows.</li></ul>"},{"title":"Light settings","body":"<p>Select the light and open the <strong>Object Data Properties</strong> tab (the green bulb). Point, Spot and Area lights use <strong>Power</strong> in watts; a Sun uses <strong>Strength</strong>. Set <em>Color</em> for warm or cool light, and for an Area light increase <em>Size</em> to soften shadows.</p>"},{"title":"A three-light setup","body":"<ul><li><strong>Key light:</strong> the main, brightest light, about 45° to one side of the camera and a little above.</li>\n<li><strong>Fill light:</strong> on the opposite side, weaker, to lift the shadows.</li>\n<li><strong>Rim light:</strong> behind the subject, outlining its edges against the background.</li></ul>\n<p>Area lights work well for all three.</p>"},{"title":"World lighting and previewing","body":"<p>The <strong>World Properties</strong> tab sets the background. Its <em>Color</em> and <em>Strength</em> add soft light from every direction. Press <kbd>Z</kbd> &gt; <strong>Rendered</strong> to see the real lighting in the viewport as you adjust it.</p>"}],"quiz":[{"q":"Which light's position doesn't change the lighting, only its rotation?","options":["Point","Sun","Spot","Area"],"answer":1,"explain":"A Sun light is treated as infinitely far away, so only its direction matters."},{"q":"How do you soften the shadows from an Area light?","options":["Lower its power","Increase its size","Change it to a Point light","Turn off the world"],"answer":1,"explain":"A larger light source produces softer shadow edges."}],"exercise":{"title":"Light the room","task":"<p>In the room scene with materials, add a key, fill and rim light, and set a dim world background. Preview in Rendered shading until the table is clearly lit with soft shadows.</p>","files":["room-scene.obj"],"solution":["Delete the default light if it's still there.","<kbd>Shift</kbd> <kbd>A</kbd> &gt; Light &gt; Area for the key light. Place it in front of the table, to one side and above; rotate it to face the table. Set Power around 300 W and Size 1 m.","Add a second Area light on the other side at about a third of the power (fill), and a third behind the table pointing towards the camera (rim).","World Properties: set a dark blue-gray Color and Strength 0.3.","Check with <kbd>Z</kbd> &gt; Rendered and adjust the powers until no part of the table is completely black."]}},{"id":"rendering","title":"Rendering a finished scene","summary":"Place a camera, choose render settings, and save a finished image of the scene you built.","diagram":{"type":"flow","title":"From camera to saved image","nodes":["Place the camera","Choose a render engine","Set the resolution","Render (F12)","Save as PNG"],"highlight":3},"steps":[{"title":"Place the camera","body":"<p>Add a camera with <kbd>Shift</kbd> <kbd>A</kbd> &gt; <strong>Camera</strong> if the scene has none. The easiest way to aim it: navigate the viewport until you like the view, then choose <strong>View &gt; Align View &gt; Align Active Camera to View</strong> (<kbd>Ctrl</kbd> <kbd>Alt</kbd> <kbd>Numpad0</kbd>). Press <kbd>Numpad0</kbd> to look through the camera. To make another camera the active one, select it and choose <strong>View &gt; Cameras &gt; Set Active Object as Camera</strong>.</p>"},{"title":"Choose a render engine","body":"<p>In <strong>Render Properties</strong>, set <em>Render Engine</em>:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>EEVEE:</strong> fast, good for previews and stylized looks.</li>\n<li><strong>Cycles:</strong> traces light physically for realistic shadows and reflections, but takes longer. Lower <em>Samples</em> and keep <em>Denoise</em> on for quicker renders.</li>\n<li><strong>Workbench:</strong> flat shading for checking models.</li></ul>"},{"title":"Set the size","body":"<p>In <strong>Output Properties</strong>, set <em>Resolution X</em> and <em>Y</em> (1920 × 1080 for a standard landscape image) and the <em>%</em> below them. Lower the percentage for quick test renders, then set it back to 100% for the final image.</p>"},{"title":"Render and save","body":"<p>Choose <strong>Render &gt; Render Image</strong> (<kbd>F12</kbd>). A window opens and fills in the image. When it's done, choose <strong>Image &gt; Save As</strong> in that window, pick PNG, and save. Save the scene itself with <strong>File &gt; Save</strong> (<kbd>Ctrl</kbd> <kbd>S</kbd>) so you can return to it.</p>"}],"quiz":[{"q":"Which command points the active camera at what you're currently looking at?","options":["View > Frame All","View > Align View > Align Active Camera to View","Object > Snap","Render > View Render"],"answer":1,"explain":"Align Active Camera to View (Ctrl+Alt+Numpad0) moves the camera to match the viewport."},{"q":"Your Cycles test renders are too slow. What's a sensible first change?","options":["Switch the light to Sun","Lower the samples and resolution percentage, keeping denoise on","Delete the materials","Turn off the camera"],"answer":1,"explain":"Fewer samples and a smaller preview size speed up test renders; denoising cleans up the noise."}],"exercise":{"title":"Render the room","task":"<p>Frame the lit room so the table fills about a third of the image, render it at 1920 × 1080 with Cycles, and save it as a PNG.</p>","files":["room-scene.obj"],"solution":["Navigate to a three-quarter view of the table, then press <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> <kbd>Alt</kbd> <kbd>Numpad0</kbd>. Press <kbd>Numpad0</kbd> to check the framing; the camera's border shows exactly what will render.","Render Properties: Render Engine Cycles, Samples about 128, Denoise on.","Output Properties: Resolution 1920 × 1080, 50% for a test.","<kbd>F12</kbd> to test, adjust, then set 100% and render again. In the render window, Image &gt; Save As &gt; PNG."]}}],"versionNote":"Software makers update their programs, so menus and button locations can change between versions. This guide is revised when an update changes the steps, and the revised version appears the next time you open it."};
